diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase diagnostic sweep of the ospf_enterprise_static lab found no fault at any layer of the stack. The user's helpdesk report described web2.local and web3.local as unreachable from host_1_1_1_1, while web0.local and web1.local worked normally.

**Affected component:** None — no subsystem, daemon, interface, or policy is misconfigured or failing.

**Mechanism:** The service_snapshot helper confirmed that, right now, both hosts (host_1_1_1_1 and host_2_1_1_1) successfully resolve all four .local hostnames via the DNS server at 10.200.0.2 (web2.local → 10.200.0.5, web3.local → 10.200.0.6), and receive HTTP 200 responses from all four web servers with sub-5 ms latency. ICMP reachability from hosts to all server IPs showed 0% loss. The OSPF fabric is fully converged across all 6 FRR routers with Full adjacencies and 10 routes each. No ACL/nftables rules block any traffic. No TC qdisc shaping is present on any interface. All four web servers answer localhost HTTP with 200. No stress tools, CPU pressure, or socket saturation were detected on any device.

**Localization:** N/A — no device is implicated.

**Root cause:** The user's reported symptom cannot be reproduced by live testing; the topology is operating correctly in all observable respects at the time of this investigation.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. If the problem was genuine but transient (e.g., a brief service restart or temporary DNS hiccup), it has self-resolved. The user should attempt to reproduce the issue and re-open the ticket if it recurs.
